{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1755 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "2459a0879228b88eddbbd4780f8f68b69c410afb6d0113d3322ca9cd670bb71d" :edb/db-before-t 1754 :edb/db-after-t 1755 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535067 50 #inst "2026-10-08T16:05:26.918Z" 13194139535067 true] [17592186161708 1100 "Which of the following could be matrices of quadratic forms defined on $\\mathbb{R}^2$?\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139535067 false] [17592186161708 1100 "Which of the following could be matrices of quadratic forms defined on $\\mathbb{R}^2?$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/assets/e51c52e07d49052f578138e5c1d7e9f84a95ba8c454e7fcf834dcc9047dad432.png)" 13194139535067 true] [17592186161708 1145 "Recall that every quadratic form defined on $\\mathbb{R}^2$ has an associated $2 \\times 2$ symmetric matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $L$ *cannot* be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on $\\mathbb{R}^2$ since it's a $3 \\times 3$ matrix (not $2 \\times 2$).\n\n- $M$ *cannot* be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on $\\mathbb{R}^2.$  Although it's a $2 \\times 2$ matrix, $M$ is not symmetric since $m_{12} \\neq m_{21}.$\n\n- $N$ could be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on $\\mathbb{R}^2$ since it is a $2 \\times 2$ symmetric matrix.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$N$ only.\"" 13194139535067 true] [17592186161708 1145 "Recall that every quadratic form defined on $\\mathbb{R}^2$ has an associated $2 \\times 2$ symmetric matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $L$ *cannot* be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on $\\mathbb{R}^2$ since it's a $3 \\times 3$ matrix (not $2 \\times 2$).\n- $M$ *cannot* be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on $\\mathbb{R}^2$. Although it's a $2 \\times 2$ matrix, $M$ is not symmetric since $m_{12} \\neq m_{21}$.\n- $N$ could be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on $\\mathbb{R}^2$ since it is a $2 \\times 2$ symmetric matrix.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$N$ only.\"" 13194139535067 false] [17592186161709 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/assets/791b214858b7ff88b6ad1ba98ea8336abbd678ffb34751b041e11cec8cd44143.png)\nGiven the matrix $A$ above, evaluate the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x}$ when ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/assets/b7df527ba563debac29dbdd838aadb4f219653a77077b70b65bdbfe579e26a0a.png)" 13194139535067 true] [17592186161709 1100 "$\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$\nGiven the matrix $A$ above, evaluate the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x}$ when $\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139535067 false] [17592186161709 1145 "Substituting ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/assets/1a8e02bec4af0b88d7fa0f4cd87b9e0f89757ca87f494e1451650ac4f3a06dcf.png) into the expression for $Q(\\mathbf{x}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}^{T}\\,Ax \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 23\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =86.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535067 true] [17592186161709 1145 "Substituting $\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$ into the expression for $Q(\\mathbf{x})$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ(x) &= x^{T}Ax \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 23\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= 86\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535067 false] [17592186161710 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/assets/59d795aef27f3ae7598e1a8503ba0dbde377486b355e0db6decc4753f9ae7544.png)\nConsider the matrix $A$ above. Which expression represents the corresponding quadratic form $\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x}$ in terms of the components of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/assets/31a4a301bddfd7435f188f76c1702beb7c2eea01a1b174d5195f321cfdef3103.png)" 13194139535067 true] [17592186161710 1100 "$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrix $A$ above. Which expression represents the corresponding quadratic form $\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x}$ in terms of the components of $\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139535067 false] [17592186161710 1145 "**Method 1**\n\nEach entry $a_{ij}$ of $A$ corresponds to the term $a_{ij}x_i x_j$ in the expression of the quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{T}Ax &= -1x_{1}x_{1} + (1)x_{1}x_{2} + (1)x_{2}x_{1} + - 1x_{2}x_{2} \\\\\n&=-x_{1}^{2} + x_{1}x_{2} + x_{2}x_{1} - x_{2}^{2} \\\\\n&=-x_{1}^{2} + 2x_{1}x_{2} - x_{2}^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo write down a quadratic form in terms of vector components, we compute the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{T}Ax &= \\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} & x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} & x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-x_{1} + x_{2} \\\\ x_{1} - x_{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= x_{1}(-x_{1} + x_{2}) + x_{2}(x_{1} - x_{2}) \\\\\n&=-x_{1}^{2} + x_{1}x_{2} + x_{2}x_{1} - x_{2}^{2} \\\\\n&=-x_{1}^{2} + 2x_{1}x_{2} - x_{2}^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535067 false] [17592186161710 1145 "**Method 1**\n\nEach entry $a_{ij}$ of $A$ corresponds to the term $a_{ij}x_i x_j$ in the expression of the quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ax & =-1{x}_{1}{x}_{1}+(1){x}_{1}{x}_{2}+(1){x}_{2}{x}_{1}+-1{x}_{2}{x}_{2} \\\\  & =-{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =-{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo write down a quadratic form in terms of vector components, we compute the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ax & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={x}_{1}(-{x}_{1}+{x}_{2})+{x}_{2}({x}_{1}-{x}_{2}) \\\\  & =-{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =-{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535067 true] [17592186161711 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/assets/1bd9859db9b628b26d250310d38222e8d1474cbfa8d5d991217dc74d04e3f47d.png)\nThe matrix of the quadratic form ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/assets/d4ecb15785c7b91e8596dd5ee6d5f4f37bf8021c5be668ec618955d401d4818f.png) is given above. What is the value of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/assets/5076087e153b1b98d9b1798601bd9f4fb4858085d9fd7b5695ee28d0ec82383c.png)" 13194139535067 true] [17592186161711 1100 "$\\begin{bmatrix}a & 0 & b \\\\ 0 & 2 & c \\\\ b & c & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nThe matrix of the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x}) = x_1^2 - 2x_1x_3 + 4x_2x_3 + 2x_2^2$ is given above. What is the value of $a+b+c$?" 13194139535067 false] [17592186161711 1145 "Since the matrix of the quadratic form must be symmetric, we rewrite our expression as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/assets/8645c292302b7edfc416cf5ca509325a6ee9381fb231a7f2b159803b4dfda807.png)\n\nNotice that to get a symmetric matrix, we must do the following splits:\n\n- we split the coefficient of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/assets/dfb385bea2635645b1f9c86af71eff0dce97a954417745dbd38490f018f08e0a.png) between the entries ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/assets/6a348fce74db828ec978c7e135e99593bc998bfabecec36ecaf759cc06dba537.png) and\n\n- we split the coefficient of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/assets/0ce406288d4b63c3156067f4ff753a5fec13d4461f11cf58252a1361f16b6911.png) between the entries ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/assets/c24c0fe76b0f877030d6130109d0b82ef187e05be9c103d8b913b9c3eb654200.png)\n\nSo, the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/assets/e8fda0676083681fa61f11e26ca5455e06cf93ce11b657f7e1b46be3c30ff899.png)\n\nTherefore, ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/assets/29e111e47c4fb83d1a5be9b51f83e463770c5c5d7a99eae453281f617e34fe86.png) ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/assets/d6faa78e35bd56fb2a138698ca72569d54c7da3305c3bb5b9dfafd8662d49d7a.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/assets/a8c65541492f0454624e520d1b86952208a1206735718dd49b7455f003d2bce5.png) Finally, \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/assets/851f6d6822dca779fa17406a68c7e6fbce37cf2642ce5994f8e95ede41e31921.png)" 13194139535067 true] [17592186161711 1145 "Since the matrix of the quadratic form must be symmetric, we rewrite our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ(x) &= x_{1}^{2} - 2x_{1}x_{3} + 4x_{2}x_{3} + 2x_{2}^{2} \\\\\n&= 1x_{1}^{2} + 0x_{1}x_{2} + (-1)x_{1}x_{3} \\\\\n&= + 0x_{2}x_{1} + 2x_{2}^{2} + 2x_{2}x_{3} \\\\\n&= + (-1)x_{3}x_{1} + 2x_{3}x_{2} + 0x_{3}^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that to get a symmetric matrix, we must do the following splits:\n\n- we split the coefficient of ${\\color{blue}-2}x_1x_3$ between the entries $a_{13}=a_{31}=\\dfrac{\\color{blue}-2}{2}=-1$, and\n- we split the coefficient of ${\\color{blue}4}x_2x_3$ between the entries $a_{23}=a_{32}=\\dfrac{\\color{blue}4}{2}=2$.\n\nSo, the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=1$, $b=-1$, and $c=2$. Finally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=1+(-1)+2 = 2\n$$" 13194139535067 false] [17592186161916 1020 #edb/ref 17592186329460 13194139535067 true] [17592186161916 1020 #edb/ref 17592186329487 13194139535067 true] [17592186161916 1020 #edb/ref 17592186329494 13194139535067 true] [17592186161916 1020 #edb/ref 17592186329501 13194139535067 true] [17592186161917 1020 #edb/ref 17592186329508 13194139535067 true] [17592186161917 1020 #edb/ref 17592186329515 13194139535067 true] [17592186161917 1020 #edb/ref 17592186329522 13194139535067 true] [17592186161917 1020 #edb/ref 17592186329529 13194139535067 true] [17592186161918 1020 #edb/ref 17592186329467 13194139535067 true] [17592186161918 1020 #edb/ref 17592186329536 13194139535067 true] [17592186161918 1020 #edb/ref 17592186329543 13194139535067 true] [17592186161918 1020 #edb/ref 17592186329550 13194139535067 true] [17592186161918 1020 #edb/ref 17592186329557 13194139535067 true] [17592186161919 1020 #edb/ref 17592186329474 13194139535067 true] [17592186161919 1020 #edb/ref 17592186329564 13194139535067 true] [17592186161919 1020 #edb/ref 17592186329571 13194139535067 true] [17592186161919 1020 #edb/ref 17592186329578 13194139535067 true] [17592186161919 1020 #edb/ref 17592186329585 13194139535067 true] [17592186162518 1017 #edb/ref 17592186160740 13194139535067 false] [17592186162518 1017 #edb/ref 17592186329481 13194139535067 true] [17592186162518 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535067 true] [17592186162518 1100 "Which of the following could be matrices of quadratic forms defined on $R^{2}$?\n\n$P = \\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}, Q = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 7 & -1 & 9\\end{bmatrix}, R = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139535067 false] [17592186162518 1100 "Which of the following could be matrices of quadratic forms defined on ${R}^{2}?$\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 7 & -1 & 9\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139535067 true] [17592186162518 1145 "Recall that every quadratic form defined on ${R}^{2}$ has an associated $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ could be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{2}$ since it is a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix.\n\n- $Q$ *cannot* be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{2}$ since it's a $3\\times 3$ matrix (not $2\\times 2$).\n\n- $R$ *cannot* be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{2}.$ Although it's a $2\\times 2$ matrix, $R$ is not symmetric since ${r}_{12}\\ne {r}_{21}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ only.\"" 13194139535067 true] [17592186162519 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535067 true] [17592186162519 1100 "Consider the quadratic form\n\n$Q(x) = x_{1}^{2} - 3x_{1}x_{3} - x_{2}x_{3} + x_{3}^{2}$.\nEvaluate $Q(x)$ when $x = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139535067 false] [17592186162519 1100 "Consider the quadratic form \n$Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}-3{x}_{1}{x}_{3}-{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+{x}_{3}^{2}.$ \nEvaluate $Q(x)$ when $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139535067 true] [17592186162519 1145 "We are given that \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the expression for $Q(x)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}_{1}^{2}-3{x}_{1}{x}_{3}-{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & ={1}^{2}-3\\cdot 1\\cdot (-4)-0\\cdot (-4)+(-4)^{2} \\\\  & =1+12+0+16 \\\\  & =29.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535067 true] [17592186329460 1017 #edb/ref 17592186329461 13194139535067 true] [17592186329460 1089 "q-140870" 13194139535067 true] [17592186329460 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535067 true] [17592186329460 1100 "Which of the following could be matrices of quadratic forms defined on ${R}^{3}?$\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,S=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,T=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139535067 true] [17592186329460 1113 #uuid "0963d160-f5ae-51a3-8391-bc24ece6fe93" 13194139535067 true] [17592186329460 1145 "Recall that every quadratic form defined on ${R}^{3}$ has an associated $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $R$ *cannot* be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{3}$ since it's a $2\\times 2$ matrix (not $3\\times 3$).\n\n- $S$ *cannot* be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{3}.$ Although it's a $3\\times 3$ matrix, $S$ is not symmetric since ${s}_{23}\\ne {s}_{32}.$\n\n- $T$ could be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{3}$ since it is a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$T$ only.\"" 13194139535067 true] [17592186329461 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329462 13194139535067 true] [17592186329461 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329463 13194139535067 true] [17592186329461 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329464 13194139535067 true] [17592186329461 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329465 13194139535067 true] [17592186329461 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329466 13194139535067 true] [17592186329461 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535067 true] [17592186329461 1041 "selection" 13194139535067 true] [17592186329461 1104 #edb/ref 17592186329462 13194139535067 true] [17592186329461 1110 #uuid "1054e6d5-cbf9-530b-89b1-b4ff3d2dcf8c" 13194139535067 true] [17592186329462 1034 "$T$ only" 13194139535067 true] [17592186329462 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535067 true] [17592186329462 1111 #uuid "4809d6b8-54c2-5c32-a52e-c040650c79a9" 13194139535067 true] [17592186329463 1034 "$S$ only" 13194139535067 true] [17592186329463 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535067 true] [17592186329463 1111 #uuid "00a531bf-e7a8-5a50-8c7f-fd27d0459d42" 13194139535067 true] [17592186329464 1034 "$S$ and $T$ only" 13194139535067 true] [17592186329464 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535067 true] [17592186329464 1111 #uuid "4c1f5581-f82a-568c-b69d-7cddc35596a2" 13194139535067 true] [17592186329465 1034 "$R$ and $S$ only" 13194139535067 true] [17592186329465 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535067 true] [17592186329465 1111 #uuid "56bf286f-55b5-5d08-b06f-f523de6e25ba" 13194139535067 true] [17592186329466 1034 "$R$ only" 13194139535067 true] [17592186329466 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535067 true] [17592186329466 1111 #uuid "088302bb-c647-5b28-9c14-bffb3a522f77" 13194139535067 true] [17592186329467 1017 #edb/ref 17592186329468 13194139535067 true] [17592186329467 1089 "q-140928" 13194139535067 true] [17592186329467 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535067 true] [17592186329467 1100 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ above. Which of the following represents the corresponding quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,Ax$ in terms of the components of $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]?$" 13194139535067 true] [17592186329467 1113 #uuid "4a4aa31a-9bd3-531e-9d58-38401292e334" 13194139535067 true] [17592186329467 1145 "**Method 1**\n\nEach entry ${a}_{ij}$ of $A$ corresponds to the term ${a}_{ij}{x}_{i}{x}_{j}$ in the expression of the quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ax & =1{x}_{1}{x}_{1}+0{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+(-1){x}_{1}{x}_{3}+0{x}_{2}{x}_{1}+0{x}_{2}{x}_{2}+3{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+(-1){x}_{3}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{3}{x}_{2}+0{x}_{3}{x}_{3} \\\\  & ={x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+3{x}_{2}{x}_{3}-{x}_{3}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{3}{x}_{2} \\\\  & ={x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+6{x}_{2}{x}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo write down a quadratic form in terms of vector components, we compute the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ax & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2} & {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2} & {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{3} \\\\ 3{x}_{3} \\\\ -{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={x}_{1}({x}_{1}-{x}_{3})+{x}_{2}(3{x}_{3})+{x}_{3}(-{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}) \\\\  & ={x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+3{x}_{2}{x}_{3}-{x}_{3}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{3}{x}_{2} \\\\  & ={x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+6{x}_{2}{x}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535067 true] [17592186329468 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329469 13194139535067 true] [17592186329468 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329470 13194139535067 true] [17592186329468 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329471 13194139535067 true] [17592186329468 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329472 13194139535067 true] [17592186329468 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329473 13194139535067 true] [17592186329468 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535067 true] [17592186329468 1041 "selection" 13194139535067 true] [17592186329468 1104 #edb/ref 17592186329473 13194139535067 true] [17592186329468 1110 #uuid "1f75f9c0-687a-5392-9af8-c1d37e52ce39" 13194139535067 true] [17592186329469 1034 "{x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+3{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+3{x}_{3}^{2}" 13194139535067 true] [17592186329469 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535067 true] [17592186329469 1111 #uuid "cd8cbfee-e53b-501e-a2a3-25444ae58ebf" 13194139535067 true] [17592186329470 1034 "{x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+3{x}_{2}^{2}+3{x}_{3}{x}_{2}" 13194139535067 true] [17592186329470 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535067 true] [17592186329470 1111 #uuid "924b1430-70aa-58a7-a5ce-247012855de3" 13194139535067 true] [17592186329471 1034 "{x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{1}{x}_{3}-{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+6{x}_{2}{x}_{3}" 13194139535067 true] [17592186329471 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535067 true] [17592186329471 1111 #uuid "309d74c0-5669-5797-97b3-75efbfec634a" 13194139535067 true] [17592186329472 1034 "{x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+3{x}_{2}{x}_{3}" 13194139535067 true] [17592186329472 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535067 true] [17592186329472 1111 #uuid "48f7ecd5-10d3-54f2-af5e-cc25baf11f52" 13194139535067 true] [17592186329473 1034 "{x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+6{x}_{2}{x}_{3}" 13194139535067 true] [17592186329473 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535067 true] [17592186329473 1111 #uuid "870b3125-b82d-5dd0-b2bf-a1d4b061049f" 13194139535067 true] [17592186329474 1017 #edb/ref 17592186329475 13194139535067 true] [17592186329474 1089 "q-140932" 13194139535067 true] [17592186329474 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535067 true] [17592186329474 1100 "$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ b & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix of the quadratic form $Q(x)=2{x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2}\\,$ is given above. What is the value of $a+b?$" 13194139535067 true] [17592186329474 1113 #uuid "46502b93-4d5f-5b44-a9ca-d570ed1198d9" 13194139535067 true] [17592186329474 1145 "Since the matrix of the quadratic form must be symmetric, we rewrite our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =2{x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =2{x}_{1}^{2}+(-2){x}_{1}{x}_{2} \\\\  & +\\,(-2){x}_{2}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that to get a symmetric matrix, we must split the coefficient of $-4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$ between the entries \n\n$$\n{a}_{12}={a}_{21}=\\frac{(-4)}{2}=-2\n$$\n\nof our matrix. So, the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=2$ and $b=-2.$ Finally, \n\n$$\na+b=2+(-2)=0.\n$$" 13194139535067 true] [17592186329475 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329476 13194139535067 true] [17592186329475 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329477 13194139535067 true] [17592186329475 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329478 13194139535067 true] [17592186329475 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329479 13194139535067 true] [17592186329475 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329480 13194139535067 true] [17592186329475 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535067 true] [17592186329475 1041 "selection" 13194139535067 true] [17592186329475 1104 #edb/ref 17592186329479 13194139535067 true] [17592186329475 1110 #uuid "dedf6fd6-47a3-5036-97a0-506ab279bd4d" 13194139535067 true] [17592186329476 1034 "2" 13194139535067 true] [17592186329476 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535067 true] [17592186329476 1111 #uuid "d34bd976-257d-5945-8a50-eceb2a407ede" 13194139535067 true] [17592186329477 1034 "6" 13194139535067 true] [17592186329477 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535067 true] [17592186329477 1111 #uuid "c8bbc316-0329-531a-98a4-1a3e1f8a28c6" 13194139535067 true] [17592186329478 1034 "4" 13194139535067 true] [17592186329478 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535067 true] [17592186329478 1111 #uuid "ccc39b96-a204-55cf-ac76-e3eb2a9b4929" 13194139535067 true] [17592186329479 1034 "0" 13194139535067 true] [17592186329479 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535067 true] [17592186329479 1111 #uuid "3979a24d-dd44-5002-a16a-2afe4b43c2c6" 13194139535067 true] [17592186329480 1034 "-2" 13194139535067 true] [17592186329480 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535067 true] [17592186329480 1111 #uuid "56d51bb0-20f2-57c0-a14e-dc4c879af00c" 13194139535067 true] [17592186329481 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329482 13194139535067 t